From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jaydens-tasty-slop
 <325336397+jaydens-tasty-slop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 07:45:22 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dsi: clear a stale clock lane HS request when
 deferring its start

With msm.defer_hs_clk=1 the host leaves the clock lane in LP-11 at
power-on and forces it to HS before the first command. dsi_ctrl_enable()
builds the new LANE_CTRL value from the register's current contents, and
nothing clears CLKLN_HS_FORCE_REQUEST at power-off: dsi_ctrl_disable()
only writes DSI_CTRL. After the first power-on the bit is still set, so
on every later power-on the clock lane is in HS again before the panel
driver releases the panel reset, the case the parameter exists to avoid.

On the Galaxy Tab S3 (Samsung AMS968HH01 panel, ANA38401 DDI) this left
the panel dark after an fb0 blank and unblank: the DDI does not answer
reads until after display-on, the same signature as without
msm.defer_hs_clk. Clearing the bit before the write fixes it, so the
bit is still set when dsi_ctrl_enable() reads the register.

Without msm.defer_hs_clk the host sets the bit at every power-on
anyway, so this changes nothing there.

The vendor driver writes 0 to LANE_CTRL at every controller init
(mdss_dsi_host_init() in drivers/video/msm/mdss/mdss_dsi_host.c,
Samsung's GPL-2.0 kernel source for the SM-T820). Clear the request bit
in the deferred path.

Tested on an SM-T820: four blank/unblank cycles in one boot; the panel
answers reads in prepare and shows the same picture after each.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
index d8abe17ab3..321ee62af6 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
@@ -940,6 +940,13 @@ static void dsi_ctrl_enable(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host,
 			lane_ctrl &= ~DSI_LANE_CTRL_HS_REQ_SEL_PHY;
 
 		if (defer_hs_clk) {
+			/*
+			 * LANE_CTRL keeps the request from the previous
+			 * power-on (the vendor driver clears the register at
+			 * every controller init): drop it, or the clock lane
+			 * is in HS again through the next panel reset.
+			 */
+			lane_ctrl &= ~DSI_LANE_CTRL_CLKLN_HS_FORCE_REQUEST;
 			dsi_write(msm_host, REG_DSI_LANE_CTRL, lane_ctrl);
 			msm_host->hs_clk_pending = true;
 		} else {
